Sure, here's the translation of the given text into UVL (Unified Version Language) code:

```uvl
-- Define a team with members
team "Security Team" {
    -- Define the roles for each member
    role "Security Architect" {
        -- Specify the required skills
        skill "Security Analysis"
        skill "Network Security"
        skill "Data Encryption"
    }

    role "Security Engineer" {
        -- Specify the required skills
        skill "Application Security"
        skill "Web Application Security"
        skill "IoT Security"
    }

    role "Manager" {
        -- Specify the required skills
        skill "Project Management"
        skill "Leadership"
        skill "Team Building"
    }

    role "Scrum Master" {
        -- Specify the required skills
        skill "Agile Project Management"
        skill "Communication Skills"
        skill "Problem Solving"
    }
}
```

In this UVL code:
- A `team` named "Security Team" is defined.
- Three `role`s are added: "Security Architect", "Security Engineer", and "Manager".
- Each role specifies the required `skill`s using the `skill` keyword.
- There are also two additional roles: "Scrum Master" and "Manager".

This structure ensures that each member of the team is assigned the appropriate role, and no two team members can have the same role.